  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 311 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unseasonably warm weather and chances for showers \nand storms start today and continue into the weekend. Then another \ncool down looks likely at the start of next week.\n\nMorning weather analysis shows an elongated 1026mb high pressure off \nthe east coast. The high pressure allows for another pleasant night \nacross the area, with sky's remaining clear to mostly clear and \ntemperatures hovering in the low to middle 60s inland and upper 60s \nto low 70s along the coast. Through the rest of the morning expect \ntemperatures to continue to drop into the upper 50s to low 60s inland \nand middle to upper 60s along the coast. Over the course of the day \ntemperatures will likely  warm back into the upper 80s to possibly \nlower 90s. However confidence in widespread 90s is low at this time \ndue to weak southerly flow. Along the coast temperatures will likely \nbe in the mid to upper 80s. In addition, a weak front will drop \nsouth out from the Ohio Valley toward the area. This will allow for \nshower chances to increase primarily across far NW portions of the \nCWA (Louisa and Fluvanna, primarily) in the afternoon. Coverage of \nthe showers looks to be very minimal due to weak forcing. A few \nshowers may linger across the far northern portion of the area into \nThursday evening with overnight lows in the mid 60s to low 70s. \n\nBy Friday, the front looks to pass through the area early in the \nmorning as a high pressure builds south out of the Great Lakes. CAMS \ncontinue to hint on pre and post-frontal showers mainly over the SW \nhalf of the area. Will note, QPF with these possible showers look to \nbe minimal (0.1 to 0.25\"). In addition to the showers, with the \nfront moving through the morning temperatures may struggle to warm \nespecially across the north. The highs are likely to be in the low \nto mid 80s north and upper 80s to possible 90F south. Mostly dry and \nsomewhat cooler on Saturday behind the front with highs in the low \nto mid 80s. The front likely lifts back to the north Sunday into \nearly next week with increasing deep moisture and chances for \ndiurnal showers and storms.\n\nLooking ahead to early next week there is a decent synoptic \nagreement from the EURO, Canadian, & GFS ensembles. Confidence has \nincreased slightly on a strong upper level ridge building over the \nHudson Bay. At the surface, this allows for the potential of a \nstrong high pressure to center itself over Eastern Canada. Looking \nat pattern recognition with the potential placement of the ridge and \nsurface high, this will likely lead to onshore flow leading to much \ncooler temperatures.",
